    ^{:line 63 :tag (* g)} g





    ^{:line 69 :tag bool :doc "dead indicates the goroutine was not suspended because it\nis dead. This goroutine could be reused after the dead\nstate was observed, so the caller must not assume that it\nremains dead.\n"} dead




    ^{:line 74 :tag bool :doc "stopped indicates that this suspendG transitioned the G to\n_Gwaiting via g.preemptStop and thus is responsible for\nreadying it when done.\n"} stopped))































^{:go/end 255} (go/func ^:go/systemstack suspendG "suspendG suspends goroutine gp at a safe-point and returns the\nstate of the suspended goroutine. The caller gets read access to\nthe goroutine until it calls resumeG.\n\nIt is safe for multiple callers to attempt to suspend the same\ngoroutine at the same time. The goroutine may execute between\nsubsequent successful suspend operations. The current\nimplementation grants exclusive access to the goroutine, and hence\nmultiple callers will serialize. However, the intent is to grant\nshared read access, so please don't depend on exclusive access.\n\nThis must be called from the system stack and the user goroutine on\nthe current M (if any) must be in a preemptible state. This\nprevents deadlocks where two goroutines attempt to suspend each\nother and both are in non-preemptible states. There are other ways\nto resolve this deadlock, but this seems simplest.\n\nTODO(austin): What if we instead required this to be called from a\nuser goroutine? Then we could deschedule the goroutine while\nwaiting instead of blocking the thread. If two goroutines tried to\nsuspend each other, one of them would win and the other wouldn't\ncomplete the suspend until it was resumed. We would have to be\ncareful that they couldn't actually queue up suspend for each other\nand then both be suspended. This would also avoid the need for a\nkernel context switch in the synchronous case because we could just\ndirectly schedule the waiter. The context switch is unavoidable in\nthe signal case.\n" ^suspendGState [^{:tag (* g)} gp]
  (when [mp (.-m (getg))] (and (!= (.-curg mp) nil) (== (readgstatus (.-curg mp)) _Grunning))
    ;; Since we're on the system stack of this M, the user
    ;; G is stuck at an unsafe point. If another goroutine
    ;; were to try to preempt m.curg, it could deadlock.
    (throw "suspendG from non-preemptible goroutine"))


  ;; See https://golang.org/cl/21503 for justification of the yield delay.
